<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nebuchadnezzar II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 562 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Nebuchadnezzar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nebuchadnezzar II was the most famous king of the Chaldean or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, who died in 562 BCE. As crown prince he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> at Carchemish in 605 BCE, securing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. As king he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> on a grand scale and invested in canals, making his reign the defining era of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii.jpg" alt="Nebuchadnezzar II" width="320" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nebuchadnezzar_II_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His Akkadian name was Nabû-kudurri-uṣur, meaning Nabu, watch over my heir. He was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a>, who ruled from 626 to 605 BCE and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> in a rebellion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. Nabopolassar's inscriptions call him his eldest son, and his own inscriptions call him Nabopolassar's true or legitimate heir. He appears to have taken part in the campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 610 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicles</a> record that he commanded an army in a mountainous region in 607 BCE. Nabopolassar died a few weeks after the victory at Carchemish, and Nebuchadnezzar, who was still in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, hurried back and was proclaimed king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in mid-August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His early reign saw few achievements and a failed invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which led some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to doubt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>'s power. In 604 BCE he took the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, whose king was carried off to Babylon and which was plundered and levelled; excavations confirm its destruction. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zedekiah">Zedekiah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> later revolted. In 587 BCE Nebuchadnezzar besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and destroyed it and the Kingdom of Judah, deporting much of its population in what became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a>. No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> sources survive for this episode, and the only known account of Judah's fall is the biblical one.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> had also rebelled, and the Book of Ezekiel describes it in 571 BCE as recently captured. A stele from Tahpanhes records an attempted invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 582 BCE, which Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a> repelled.</p><p>Nebuchadnezzar greatly expanded and rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, a task made possible by an economy sustained by his conquests. His inscriptions record work on many temples, including the restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>, the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, and the completion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a> ziggurat. He restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-palace">South Palace</a>, built a new North Palace outside the walls facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a>, rebuilt the Processional Street and the gate itself, and built a Summer Palace north of the inner city walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 562 BCE after 43 years on the throne, the longest reign of his dynasty, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> remembered him favourably. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amel-marduk">Amel-Marduk</a> succeeded him, though their relationship may have been poor. Because only one of his fifty or so known inscriptions deals with military action, historians such as Wolfram von Soden long saw him chiefly as a builder. The publication of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a> by Donald Wiseman in 1956 shifted attention to his wars. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-jeremiah">Book of Jeremiah</a> calls him a lion and a destroyer of nations. According to tradition he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens of Babylon</a> for his wife Amytis, but no archaeological evidence for them has been found.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nebuchadnezzar II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Battle of Carchemish in 605 BCE, won by the crown prince <b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b> against the Egyptian army of Pharaoh Necho II, secured the Levant and turned a regional kingdom into the dominant power of the ancient Near East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Assyria fell in 612 BCE the island passed under Babylonian control, and by the reign of <b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b> or his successors it was ruled by a Babylonian governor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b> took Jerusalem in 587 or 586 BCE, the Edomites may have helped plunder the city and slaughter the Judaeans, and Edom may have betrayed a treaty with Judah; Edomite settlement in southern Judah and the Negev had already begun, partly by military means.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Jerusalem survived, but Judah later fell to the armies of <b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b>, who besieged the city in 597 and again in 588–587 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…in turn to Assyrians, Babylonians, Egyptians, Persians, Greeks and Romans; its position on a promontory with two harbours did not save it from <b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b>, who made it part of the Neo-Babylonian Empire, nor from the Achaemenid Empire, which took the Phoenician cities in the 6th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b> and Nabonidus of Babylon built there in the 6th century BCE, but after Babylonia fell to the Achaemenid Persians around 530 BCE Ur declined, and by the early 5th century BCE it was uninhabited, perhaps through drought, shifting rivers and the silting of the Gulf outlet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.33,45.64&amp;year=-572" aria-label="Uruk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5728 1022 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nebuchadnezzar II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6178 1292h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6178 1292h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.33,45.64&amp;year=-572">What was here in 572 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
